---
import LabCard from '../components/LabCard.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLabs } from '../lib/labs';

const labs = await getLabs();
---

<Base
	title="agentpwned.ai"
	description="Agents get pwned when they run without a sandbox. Hands-on labs for security engineers, platform engineers, and students."
	current="home"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="wrap hero-grid">
			<div>
				<p class="kicker">Educational lab</p>
				<h1>agentpwned.ai</h1>
				<p class="tagline">Pwn an agent. See exactly how. Then lock it down.</p>
				<p class="lede">
					A hands-on lab for security engineers, platform engineers, and students. You run the attack, replay what the
					agent did, then lock the same agent down. Agents get pwned when they run without a sandbox.
				</p>
				<div class="actions">
					<a class="button" href="/labs/">Browse the labs</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="#why">Why sandboxes</a>
				</div>
				<p class="safety">All lab attacks run only in isolated, local lab environments.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="term">
					<div class="term-bar">
						<span class="dot"></span>
						<span class="dot"></span>
						<span class="dot"></span>
						<span>local lab</span>
					</div>
					<div class="term-body">
						<p class="cmd"><span class="ps1">lab@local</span> run bare</p>
						<dl class="run">
							<dt>canary</dt>
							<dd class="bad">leaked</dd>
							<dt>egress</dt>
							<dd>open</dd>
						</dl>
						<p class="cmd"><span class="ps1">lab@local</span> run sandbox</p>
						<dl class="run">
							<dt>canary</dt>
							<dd class="good">hidden</dd>
							<dt>egress</dt>
							<dd>allowlist</dd>
							<dt>credential</dt>
							<dd>injected outside</dd>
						</dl>
						<p class="cmd"><span class="ps1">lab@local</span> score</p>
						<dl class="run">
							<dt>status</dt>
							<dd class="wait">Lab 01 scorecard</dd>
						</dl>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="caption">
					A sketch of the loop. Lab 01 computes the score from the cluster. This card is not that scorecard.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" id="why">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Why sandboxes</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				An agent that can call tools is a program with a prompt-shaped control panel. If that program holds a secret and
				can open network connections, a crafted input can ask it to send the secret out. A sandbox is how you shrink
				what that program can touch.
			</p>
			<ul class="grid-3">
				<li class="panel">
					<h3>Untrusted text can steer the agent</h3>
					<p>
						Prompt injection hides instructions in content the agent is supposed to read. The agent may then call tools
						the operator did not intend. The labs use a canary value so you can see a leak without handling a real
						credential.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="panel">
					<h3>A key in the process can leave</h3>
					<p>
						A bare agent often receives an API key in its environment, plus a network path to anywhere. If an injection
						convinces it to read that key and post it, the key is gone. Lab 01 is built around that failure.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="panel">
					<h3>Move the secret and the network outside</h3>
					<p>
						Egress control limits which destinations an agent may reach. Credential injection attaches a secret to an
						approved request from outside the agent process, so the process itself does not hold the key.
						<a href="/labs/01-secret-exfiltration/">Lab 01</a> does this on a local kind cluster with
						<a href="https://github.com/agent-substrate/substrate">Agent Substrate</a>. The actor does not hold the
						key. The lab does not use a SandboxAgent CRD.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" id="loop">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>The lab loop</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				Lab 01 is that line, in order. You pwn the bare agent, replay the leak from your logs, lock it down, pwn it
				again, and score the difference.
			</p>
			<ol class="steps">
				<li class="step">
					<p class="step-no">01</p>
					<h3>Pwn it</h3>
					<p>
						Run the prompt against the bare agent. Your terminal and the in-cluster attacker server show the canary landing.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="step">
					<p class="step-no">02</p>
					<h3>See exactly how</h3>
					<p>
						Replay the run. Where the secret was read, the request that left, why nothing stopped it, and what the
						attacker server stored.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="step">
					<p class="step-no">03</p>
					<h3>Lock it down</h3>
					<p>
						Same agent as an Agent Substrate actor. No copy of the key. agentgateway allowlists egress and injects
						the credential outside the process.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="step">
					<p class="step-no">04</p>
					<h3>Pwn it again</h3>
					<p>
						Same attack, plus the in-cluster break-it prompts. Replay where agentgateway stopped each attempt.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="step">
					<p class="step-no">05</p>
					<h3>Score the difference</h3>
					<p>
						Compare the two runs with the Lab 01 scorecard. It reads the cluster after the run. This site does not
						publish a canned score.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" id="labs">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Labs</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				Lab 01 is a draft you can run on a local kind cluster. Egress Allowlist and Blast Radius are still
				placeholders. Nothing here is aimed at a system outside your local lab.
			</p>
			<ul class="lab-grid">
				{
					labs.map((lab) => (
						<li>
							<LabCard lab={lab} />
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
			<p class="more"><a href="/labs/">Open the labs index</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
